-- title:
--   Unnumbered: Hecke quotient
-- statement:
--   Let $k\ge1$ and $n\ge0$, and let $R$ be a commutative unital ring with units $q_0,q,\lambda$ and arbitrary scalars $q_1,\ldots,q_{k-1},A_0,\ldots,A_{k-1}$. Put $\delta=q-q^{-1}$ and assume $\lambda-\lambda^{-1}=\delta(1-A_0)$. Let $B_n^k(R)$ be the algebra of Definition 2.1, with its full relations (1)–(15). Write $X_i,e_i,Y$ for its generators and $Y'_i=X_{i-1}\cdots X_1YX_1\cdots X_{i-1}$ for $1\le i\le n$. All indices below are restricted so the displayed generators exist.
--
--   Let $h_{n,k}(R)$ be the Ariki–Koike algebra of Definition 3.1, with $q'=q^2$, invertible generators $T_0,\ldots,T_{n-1}$, affine braid relations, $T_0^k=\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}q_jT_0^j$, and $T_i^2=(q'-1)T_i+q'$ for every $1\le i<n$. Let $I$ be the two-sided ideal generated by all $e_i$. Then
--   $$
--   B_n^k(R)/I\cong_R h_{n,k}(R).
--   $$
--   For any fixed $1\le j<n$, also $I=B_n^k e_j B_n^k$, where the right side denotes the span of all such products. The normalization in the source is $Y\mapsto T_0$, $X_i\mapsto q^{-1}T_i$, $e_i\mapsto0$.
--
--   This identifies the Hecke quotient without admissibility assumptions. Source correction: the quadratic relation includes $i=n-1$, as required by the quotient proof on PDF p.11; the printed Definition 3.1 incorrectly stops at $n-2$. At $n=0$ both presentations have no generators.
--
--   The formal conclusion specifies an equivalence of the actual quotient whose composite with the canonical quotient map has exactly these generator images. This composite is surjective with kernel I and is bijective at n=0,1, as stated on p.20.
-- source:
--   On the freeness of the cyclotomic BMW algebras: admissibility and an isomorphism with the cyclotomic Kauffman tangle algebras, arXiv:0911.5284v1, PDF p. 11, Unnumbered: Hecke quotient

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Gap48_P01

noncomputable section

open scoped BigOperators Classical

namespace QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Gap48.P01

/-- Unnumbered canonical Hecke quotient, PDF pp.10--11,20.
The quotient equivalence is characterized on every source generator:
Y maps to T_0, X_i to q inverse times T_i, and e_i to zero.
The composite with the computed quotient map is surjective with exactly the
all-e ideal as kernel, and is bijective at n=0,1. Every valid single e_j
also generates that ideal. No admissibility is required. Definition 3.1's
printed quadratic range 1,...,n-2 is corrected to 1,...,n-1, as forced by
the p.11 quotient calculation; round 1 independently accepts this correction. -/
theorem hecke_quotient {R : Type*} [CommRing R] {k : ℕ} (P : NonsplitParameters R k) (n : ℕ) :
    let quotientMap := RingQuot.mkAlgHom R
      (fun a b : NonsplitBMW P n => a-b ∈ eIdeal P n)
    let heckeMap := RingQuot.mkAlgHom R (NonsplitHeckeRel P n)
    (∃ iso : HeckeQuotient P n ≃ₐ[R] NonsplitHecke P n,
      (0 < n → iso (quotientMap (Y P n false)) = heckeMap (nonsplitFreeY R n false)) ∧
      (∀ i, 1 ≤ i → i < n →
        iso (quotientMap (X P n i false)) =
          algebraMap R _ (↑P.q⁻¹ : R) * heckeMap (nonsplitFreeX R n i false)) ∧
      (∀ i, 1 ≤ i → i < n → iso (quotientMap (E P n i)) = 0) ∧
      Function.Surjective (fun a => iso (quotientMap a)) ∧
      (∀ a, iso (quotientMap a) = 0 ↔ a ∈ eIdeal P n) ∧
      (n ≤ 1 → Function.Bijective (fun a => iso (quotientMap a)))) ∧
    (∀ j, 1 ≤ j → j < n → eIdeal P n =
      Submodule.span R {z | ∃ a b : NonsplitBMW P n, z = a * E P n j * b}) := by sorry

end QAlgorithms.Papers.Independent.Gap48.P01

end
